 on the subject of advice there  are already people anecdotally who are  going out and mixing and matching  vaccines if one of those people was a  patient of yours what would you tell  them  what i would tell them is number one  sometimes you've got to make  individualized decisions in consultation  with your healthcare provider but we  should generally whenever we can try to  follow the advice of the fda and the cdc  here because they are looking at the  totality of the data and they're looking  not just at efficacy you know is this  actually working but they're looking at  safety as well and so those  considerations have to be part of how we  make our decisions but ultimately people  have questions on mixing and matching  they should talk to their their doctor  about it there might be some reason  people don't want to stick with the  original vaccine they got maybe it has  to do with their reaction they had maybe  there are other considerations but by  and large i would follow the fda and cdc  recommendations on mixing and matching  right now and they are going to render a  final decision on that in the coming  weeks  dr murphy if we're seeing covet cases um  hospitalizations and deaths go down has  the worst been behind us  well i'm i'm an optimist by nature and  i'm cautiously optimistic that the  decline in cases and hospitalizations  that we're seeing  is something that portends more more  good news to come but we've got to be  cautious because we have seen declines  before and then we have seen cases go up  again one thing we've learned though  over this past experience particularly  with the delta wave that we've had over  the past weeks is that the vaccines have  been remarkably effective at keeping  people out of the hospital and saving  their lives we have to take that lesson  and build on it and ensure that we take  the weeks and months ahead to vaccinate  as quickly as possible the over 65  million people who remain unvaccinated  that is our surest path